editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/B5-RECONCILIATION-APPLIED.md:161:**B3 correction (supersedes §2 B3 and §5 item 1 above).** The first pass reasoned that B5's confluence "sits at the year's turn, in the deep cold, which would fall after the cache in B6's order". That is wrong. In B5 the confluence sat at d107–109, about five weeks before the cold term (d119), which is the first term's close in B6's shape, i.e. M5, not M6 (audit §F.3 item 1; B5 STATE_LEDGER:813). The gap to B6's M4 slot ("the first term's middle to late weeks") is a few weeks, not a season. So "the lighter, earlier card of a non-qualifying year" is a harmless default, not a necessity; the owner may instead keep the meet at B5's slot by letting it fall at the first term's close, and nothing else moves. The cross-tier host-exhibition filing for Lira's Silver bout is confirmed (#39 and B5's own practice).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:9:It could not, and he knew it could not; he had sat on a hundred inquiries into a hundred seals and never seen a petition refused because a press was old. But the question had come up in him on its own, from somewhere under the morning, and an evaluator who will not listen to a foolish question has only taught himself to stop hearing his instruments. So he put the petition down and went looking for where the question had come from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:110:By noon there was a refusal in *done* that had taken him a quarter of an hour to write, where such things usually took him a quarter of a minute. A guild hall had asked for more time to answer a finding on its own assessors, and he had said no; but he had said it at length, giving the hall the section it had failed to meet, the officer it might apply to instead, and the date by which it should. He had signed it with his name entire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/EVENT-LIST.md:61:4. **The coach to Ostrand.** Two hours; the first fixity proposal arrives a mile short of the city at the toll-post's bar — a courteous offer, felt and refused. The city; the wool exchange's side door; the coach in the yard; **four minutes** by Hesk's clock. Lira wants it on the seat's record; Brom asks who could do anything with it; Seln says the driver will tell the stables; Karis: no faces, no names, no paper, five witnesses who are the respondent's own and a coachman in the house's pay, on the morning the seat asked for him in person — "It reads as manufactured theater." Cael agrees last. They go in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/EVENT-LIST.md:73:6. **Day 84, naming.** Toward the second day's morning, a proposal at the window latch; tired, he names the thing instead of refusing it — *latch; it opens* — and the offer goes quiet as if answered. He tries it on the jamb: *door*. On Brom: *Brom*. It works: the new thing stops asking about anything he has named. The idle state is naming, cheap and constant, every threshold, for the rest of his life; doorways never quite stop wanting. Karis's curve: forty, thirty-six, thirty, twenty-two… two days against her nine weeks; each new thing has come in faster than the last; she writes *for now* under the line and draws a box round it. The district seat's answer comes up the hill and says exactly what Karis said a week ago. Log, the price beside the priced: what was priced, what was paid, what was not on the leaf (the doorway).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-005/EVENT-LIST.md:81:5. **Hesk.** Bracken's pouch brought a parcel at noon; Cael opens it here: a satchel of brown leather, a brass buckle Hesk filed himself, a pocket cut to the exact size of Hesk's book. The note (verbatim, Tier A). Cael reads it twice and puts the book in the pocket; it fits.
