    97	---
    98	
    99	What he did next was the thing they had come for and the thing none of them had dared predict, and he did not once let it become an alliance.
   100	
   101	He did not offer one. He did not touch the exhibits again — a small thing, and Cael charted it: a man declining to handle evidence a second time, because handling it twice was a way of making it his, and he was not making it his. He did not ask where the cache lived, which was the question the Compact's enforcement officer existed to ask, and the not-asking was the loudest thing he did all evening.
   102	
   103	And he did not reach for a pen.
   104	
   105	He told them, in the register of a man reading his own evaluation aloud, exactly what he could and could not do, and he built it the way Karis built findings, each course bearing the next.
   106	
   107	**He could not verify it.** "Every instrument I have to test this with runs on the thing it indicts. I could take the maintenance leaf to the frames I have certified for thirty years and ask them whether the sub-layer contains an advisory corrections function, and they would answer through the sub-layer, and I would be asking the accused to testify through the mechanism of the accusation." He let that stand a moment. "There is a version of me, twenty years younger, who does it anyway, on the theory that an honest question is never wasted. That man would have got a clean return in four days and believed it, and filed his belief, and been the instrument's best witness against the truth. There is no verification available to me that would not be theater, and theater, once performed, would destroy you, and him" — a nod, not looked at, toward Seln — "and me, and change nothing. I will not perform it."
   108	
   109	**He would not report it.** He said the sentence flatly and it landed in the room the way a stone lands in a well, and Cael understood while it was still in the air that he was watching a man cross a line he could not come back over: an Archmarshal, in knowing possession of evidence of the falsification, filing nothing. It was the same crossing a Shadow Path clerk had made at a coastal posting nineteen years earlier and had gone on making every morning for a decade in true empty reports, and now it had been made twice, in two registers, at two altitudes, by the only two men in the machine who had ever had the chance.
