   111	"I am required, by every oath I have taken, to report what I have seen tonight," Vastin said. "I have taken those oaths to an institution that has, I now know, been forging the documents it swore me to. An oath to a forger is not an oath." He said the last of it without any weight at all. "I will not report it. That is not a favor to you. It is the first honest thing my office has permitted me in thirty years, and I am keeping it for myself."
   112	
   113	He stopped there for a moment, and what came next was the only unbuilt thing he said all evening.
   114	
   115	"I have conducted, or supervised, or certified the frames for, somewhere above nine thousand evaluations." He said the number the way Karis said numbers, which was without any weight on it at all. "I do not know how many of those readings were corrected under me. Neither do you. Neither does anybody. That is not a rhetorical flourish; it is the actual condition, and I would like it stated plainly once tonight and then not returned to, because I am going to be living in it. I have been the most careful evaluator on this continent for thirty years, and carefulness was the *qualification*. A careless man would have produced noise. I produced clean readings, at a high rate, with a reputation that made them unquestionable, into a layer that rewrote the ones it did not like before anybody saw them." A pause. "If I had been worse at my work, I would have been less useful to it."
