   135	"Nothing you can spend." He looked at her with something that was almost respect and was certainly recognition. "It buys a line in a record that will be read in thirty years by somebody like you. I have spent my career learning that this is not nothing, and I have also spent it learning that it is not much. Both of those are true and I decline to choose between them."
   136	
   137	Lira had come up from the street at the half-hour, per the arrangement, and she asked it, because it was hers to ask.
   138	
   139	"Are you with us?"
   140	
   141	"No. I am not with anyone." He looked at her, and then at the whole table, without unkindness — an evaluator delivering a finding about himself. "For thirty years I was an instrument. I have just learned what I was an instrument *of*. A man should sit with that before he chooses what he becomes next, and I have not sat with it yet, because I read it an hour ago. You are asking me to convert the largest thing I have ever learned into a side in a fight, tonight, before I have finished learning it. I will not do that either. It would be another man lending his hands to a cause because a cause was in front of him, and I have just finished being that man."
   142	
   143	He stood.
   144	
   145	"I will do the things I told you. I will do them because they are what an honest evaluator does, not because they are what your friends need — and if you cannot tell the difference, you have not understood the only thing I came here to be."
   146	
