   164	
   165	*The Compact's best instrument just took itself out of the machine's hand — quietly, completely, without switching sides, because the truth didn't give him a side. It gave him a mirror. He read the maintenance records of his own life for an hour and then told us that turning what he'd learned into our cause tonight would be the same failure that made him an instrument in the first place, and he's right, and it's the most honest thing anyone has said to me all term, and it came from the man the machine benched for measuring true.*
   166	
   167	*He won't verify it — it would be theater. He won't report it — an oath to a forger isn't an oath. And he won't sign the certification the corridor needs — the one sentence they still require of him, withheld, so the silence reads. Three refusals. No alliance. He's still inside the Compact tonight, and he'll be inside it when this term ends, and he is more dangerous to them than any ally of mine could be, because an ally can be discredited and a serving Archmarshal quietly declining to lend his name cannot.*
   168	
   169	*Write the date. Somewhere above him, the machine has no idea it just lost the only part of itself I ever respected — not to us. To a mirror.*

