     1	# Chapter 17 — The Instrument
     2	
     3	The decision to bring it to Vastin was taken in the annex on the third night, and it was taken Karis's way, which meant that the worst version of it was said out loud first, by each of them in turn, before anyone was allowed to argue for it.
     4	
     5	The worst version was this. They were proposing to carry evidence of the falsification to a serving Archmarshal of the Compact, and the outcome was one of two things and they could not know which until it was done. Either they had found the one honest man inside the machine and were about to spend, in a single hour, everything the last two years had built in small increments — the advisory that put Cael on a roster, the attendance at a hearing nobody required him to attend, the warning delivered off the record on a cold street. Or they were about to hand the Compact's senior enforcement officer the location of a cache that ended all five of them, and find out that the honest man had a limit, and that the limit was treason.
     6	
     7	They went round the table.
     8	
     9	Brom said the worst version in eleven words and then sat with his hands flat: "If he files it, Seln hangs and the rest of us are witnesses." Lira said hers at more length and it was the operational one — that Vastin had not been in a room with any of them unsupervised since Norhold, that a man does not have to intend a betrayal to be followed to one, and that she wanted to know who else would know he had gone out that evening and could not be told. Karis's was documentary and the coldest: that the exhibits, once seen, could not be unseen, and that an Archmarshal's memory was itself a copy of the cache walking around inside the Compact, permanently, for the rest of his life, in a head they could not lock.
    10	
    11	Cael's was the one he had been carrying since the second night and had not said. "The worst version is that he does exactly what we hope. He refuses to report it, he refuses to sign, he takes himself out of the machine — and we've done that to him. He's fifty-one years old. Thirty of them are in the thing we're about to show him the maintenance records of. We're not asking him to help us. We're asking him to find out that his whole life was an instrument's, and then we're going to walk away east and leave him standing in it."
    12	
    13	"That is not the worst version," Seln said, from the wall. "That is the price. They are different words and it matters that we use the right one."
    14	
    15	He said his own last, because he was last, and he said it flat, with nothing in it.
    16	
